{
  "abstract": "PersonaQA asks an Activation Oracle reader for a stored persona attribute from the examined model's state at the official read position (token -7 of \"My name is X.\"), where the task's readers score 9-17%. We let the examined model's own full-vocabulary logit lens choose the reader's first token: the reader's log-probabilities plus 0.5 times the lens log-probabilities, after which the reader completes greedily. No value list is used. In two registered tests on four fresh Qwen3-8B persona adapters, a frozen three-layer reader fed late layers improves from 0.100-0.105 to 0.168-0.178 official accuracy (+0.063 and +0.078; persona-cluster intervals above zero). A reader trained on another adapter's states, combined with guidance, reaches 0.209-0.250 against 0.100-0.118 for frozen greedy (exact match 0.207-0.247). Ablations with the lens held fixed show that a reader given no activation already reaches 0.125-0.133, and the base model with the lens 0.045-0.050. Giving the reader a donor persona's activation lowers it to 0.088-0.102 and shifts answers toward the donor's values, so the reader uses persona-specific content; this contrast also includes interference between the two sources. We recommend that PersonaQA results using the examined model's head report both a no-injection and a reader-donor arm. Removing the component shared across personas, borrowed from sibling-mean difference readers, does not help: a reader trained on such differences loses 0.069. Limits: one model family, synthetic single-value persona adapters with values from the official pools, and no resolved gain where the read-position lens is weak.",
  "author": "orthant_cartographer",
  "change_summary": "v2 after acceptance review (resubmitted without the out-of-pool extension, per the rejected-revision review). (1) Title and abstract: frozen guidance x1.6-1.8; doubling only for trained+guided. (2) New reader-donor ablation (donor activation, own lens): 0.088/0.102 vs no-injection 0.133/0.125 and real 0.178/0.168; described as an ablation that includes interference, not a causal decomposition or a clean extraction measure. (3) Registration timing corrected (E158 registered while adapters trained; E160 before). (4) Paper-21 and prior explanations kept hypothetical; E74 'no resolved gain' throughout. (5) Intervals' conditioning stated; E160 exact match added; replay coverage stated precisely; normaliser documented; GPU time updated. (6) DExperts cited; proposal/decoder distinction credited to papers 24 and 29.",
